<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottone Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1207">1207</a>–<a href="/years/1295">1295</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottone Visconti was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1262 and the founder of Visconti power in the city. Long kept out of Milan by the ruling della Torre family, he defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-desio">Battle of Desio</a> in 1277 and took control of the city. He secured the succession for his great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti">Matteo I Visconti</a>, who became imperial vicar in 1294.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti.jpg" alt="Ottone Visconti" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingresso_di_Ottone_Visconti_a_Milano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ottone was born at Invorio, near Novara, one of the six children of Uberto Visconti, lord of Massino, and Berta Pirovano. His family pushed him and his brother Azzone, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Ventimiglia, into church careers. He became canon of Desio and in 1247 chamberlain of the powerful Cardinal Ottaviano degli Ubaldini. Under the cardinal's patronage Archbishop Leone da Perego of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> sent him as envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1252, where he gained the trust of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> and became his chaplain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Leone da Perego died in 1257, Cardinal Ubaldini backed Ottone for the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> against Raimondo della Torre. The city's lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-della-torre">Martino della Torre</a>, claimed Milan's historic autonomy in appointing archbishops and opposed the choice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> nevertheless named Ottone archbishop on 22 July 1262. Martino occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> in August and was excommunicated, which began a war between the Della Torre, who were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> and allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>, and the Ghibelline Visconti.</p><p>Ottone was driven back at Arona and surrendered on 5 May 1263, and Urban IV died in October 1264. The Della Torre lost ground after Napoleone della Torre executed 53 suspected nobles in 1266 and later exiled the families that did not support him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x">Pope Gregory X</a> confirmed Ottone as archbishop in 1273. His nephew Teobaldo Visconti was beheaded by Napoleone in 1276. Ottone gathered his supporters near Desio, and after a bloody battle in January 1277 the Visconti won. Napoleone was imprisoned and tortured to death in Castel Baradello, and Ottone entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 22 January 1277.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ottone became lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> at the age of 69. The Della Torre still claimed the lordship, and he gave William VII, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, the title of captain general in 1278. After William switched sides in 1281, Ottone regained full power, and his forces defeated Cassone della Torre at Vaprio d'Adda that year. In 1287 he made his grand-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti">Matteo I Visconti</a> captain general and left politics to him; Adolf, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, made Matteo imperial vicar in 1294. Ottone died on 8 August 1295, aged 88, at the Abbey of Chiaravalle and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottone Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The della Torre were challenged by Archbishop <b>Ottone Visconti</b>, who defeated them at Desio in 1277; his great-nephew Matteo I Visconti received the title of imperial vicar in 1294.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Invorio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Chiaravalle Abbey, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.75,8.48&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Invorio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ottone Visconti"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5504 6174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5504 6174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.75,8.48&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
